​1. Evolution of Computers & Purpose
​Why Computers? Computers were originally created to perform calculations and computations.  
​Historical Progression of Calculation Tools:
​Fingers  
​Stones & sticks  
​Abacus  
​Mechanical calculators  
​Modern computers (electronic devices executing computations based on user instructions)  
​2. Working & Core Features of a Computer
​Working Framework: Operates on an Input \rightarrow Process \rightarrow Result cycle (e.g., inputting 10 + 20 \rightarrow processing \rightarrow output result 30).  
​Features of a Computer: Automation, Speed, Accuracy, Versatility, Storage, and Connectivity.  
​3. Programming Languages & Categories
​Programming Language: A set of instructions given to a computer.  
​Categories:
​Low-Level Programming: Uses binary code (0 and 1).  
​0: OFF (current is not passing).  
​1: ON (electricity/current is passing through the circuit).  
​High-Level Programming: Written in English-like, human-understandable, and user-friendly syntax.  
​Examples: Java, .NET, Python, PHP.  
​Mid-Level Programming: Combines features of both low-level and high-level programming.  
​4. Language Translators (Compiler vs. Interpreter)
​Translators convert high-level programming code into low-level machine code:  
​Compiler:
​Described as strict.  
​Translates the entire code at once and checks for errors across the whole block simultaneously.  
​Interpreter:
​Described as friendly.  
​Processes and executes code line by line.  
​5. Software Engineering & Application Roles
​Why Software is Booming: Driven by everyday applications fulfilling end-user needs (e.g., WhatsApp for communication, Instagram to connect with people, Swiggy to fulfill hunger, Telegram for sharing notes/movies).  
​Software/Application Definition: A software program designed to execute specific tasks and provide functional value to end users.  
​3 Key Application Stages & Specialist Roles:
​Development: Building the application (handled by developers/software engineers).  
​Maintenance: Keeping the application running smoothly (handled by DevOps Engineers, Quality Assurance (QA), and Database Administrators (DBA)).  
​Security: Protecting applications from hackers and fraudulent activity (handled by Cybersecurity Specialists, Application Security Engineers, and Ethical Hackers).  
​6. Types of Applications
​Mobile Applications: Installed on mobile platforms (Android / iOS) like WhatsApp and Instagram.  
​Desktop Applications: Standalone software like MS Word, Excel, and Paint.  
​Embedded Applications: Built into hardware devices like washing machines, ACs, and smartwatches.  
​Web Applications: Accessed inside a web browser using the internet (e.g., Amazon, Flipkart).  
​7. Full-Stack Development Architecture & Technologies
​A Full-Stack Developer builds web applications end-to-end.  
​The 3 Layers of Full-Stack Architecture:
• Front-End (Client-Side)
	• Purpose: The user interface (UI) and everything the user directly sees and interacts with in their browser (buttons, forms, layout).
	• Restaurant Analogy: The Hotel/Dining Area (where customers sit, view the menu, and place orders).
	• Common Technologies: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Vue, or Tailwind.
• Back-End (Server-Side)
	• Purpose: Business logic that processes data, handles authentication, and manages requests behind the scenes.
	• Restaurant Analogy: The Kitchen (where chefs take orders, cook the food, and prepare the response).
	• Common Technologies: Node.js, Express, Python (Django/Flask), or Java.
• Database (Data Layer)
	• Purpose: Stores, organizes, and retrieves all application data (such as user accounts, product catalogs, and saved records).
	• Restaurant Analogy: The Storehouse / Pantry (where ingredients and supplies are stocked and kept safe).
	• Common Technologies: MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, or SQLite.
8.Client-Server Architecture & MVC Pattern
​Client-Server Architecture: Illustrates the lifecycle of a request (e.g., Instagram Registration)—where the user fills a form on the Front-End UI \rightarrow sends data to the Back-End application layer for validation/processing \rightarrow interacts with the Database storage layer \rightarrow and returns a success response.  
​MVC Pattern (Model-View-Controller): A software architectural pattern used to separate application concerns into Model (data/logic), View (UI/presentation), and Controller (handles user input/requests).  
